            <ArticleTitle>A review of the effects of medication delivery systems on treatment adherence in children with asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>34-44</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/S0011-393X(03)00002-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">A patient's adherence to an appropriate treatment regimen is necessary to minimize morbidity and mortality associated with childhood asthma. Many factors influence the success of treatment adherence.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The goal of this article was to examine the effect of the mode of medication delivery on the success of treatment adherence in children with asthma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Relevant clinical studies were identified through a MEDLINE search of articles published from 1966 to 2002, using the search terms adherence, aerosol, asthma, children, compliance, dry powder inhaler, metered-dose inhaler, nebulizer, and pediatric.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A relationship seems to exist between treatment adherence and the type of medication delivery system used in childhood asthma. The highest rates of adherence appear to be associated with oral medications.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Clinicians should consider the mode of medication delivery as 1 factor that can influence the success of treatment adherence.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
